### Most Used Git Commands & Why People Use Them

Here are the essential Git commands developers use daily, grouped by what they accomplish:

#### 1. Repository Setup & Syncing

* **`git clone <url>`** — Downloads an existing project from GitHub to your computer. Used when starting work on an existing repository.
* **`git pull`** — Fetches the latest changes from GitHub and merges them into your local workspace. Used to keep your local code up to date with teammates or remote updates.
* **`git push`** — Uploads your local commits to GitHub. Used to save your work online or share it with others.

#### 2. Branching & Context Switching

* **`git branch`** — Lists all local branches in your repository and shows which one you are currently on.
* **`git checkout -b <branch-name>`** (or `git switch -c <branch-name>`) — Creates a new branch and switches to it immediately. Used to isolate new features or experiments without breaking working code.
* **`git checkout <branch-name>`** (or `git switch <branch-name>`) — Switches from your current branch to an existing one.

#### 3. Staging & Saving Changes

* **`git status`** — Shows modified, newly created, or deleted files that haven't been committed yet. Used constantly to check what has changed.
* **`git add .`** — Stages all modified and new files in your project directory so they are ready to be saved.
* **`git commit -m "message"`** — Takes a snapshot of your staged files and attaches a log message. Used to save a distinct unit of work in history.

#### 4. Merging & History

* **`git merge <branch-name>`** — Combines changes from the specified branch into your current active branch.
* **`git log`** — Shows the history of all commits made in the repository.
* **`git stash`** — Temporarily saves your uncommitted changes in the background so you can switch branches cleanly without committing half-finished work. Run `git stash pop` later to restore them.

